---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign';
const title = 'Drip vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Drip and ActiveCampaign for Shopify drops, SMS access, sales follow-up, identity, permission, purchase exits and campaign economics.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Drip fit?",
    "answer": "Drip can fit an ecommerce team building email journeys around shopping behavior. Its current SMS rollout has access and identity restrictions; confirm activation for your account before assigning drop texts to it."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does ActiveCampaign fit?",
    "answer": "ActiveCampaign can fit a team joining store journeys to sales follow-up. Purchase eligible SMS and CRM capabilities separately where required, and verify the exact triggers and actions in the intended plan."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify marketing status authorize every channel?",
    "answer": "No. Preserve purpose-specific permission and capture evidence separately for email and promotional SMS. A phone number, order or synced contact is not permission to text."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will a purchase or won deal stop every reminder?",
    "answer": "Only if the workflow explicitly checks the relevant current state. Recheck the matching Shopify order and current deal before sending, including after a delay, retry or manual reassignment."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should Sequenzy be added?",
    "answer": "Only for a defined email responsibility that justifies another system. Drip and ActiveCampaign already support email; choose one owner per journey and reconcile permission, exits and reporting if a separate sender is introduced."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should the costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Use current account quotes, eligible contacts, message segments, carrier fees, CRM add-ons and operating effort. Compare incremental contribution against an eligible-customer holdout, with discounts and refunds counted once."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Drip vs ActiveCampaign</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Choose around the job: commerce email journeys with eligible Drip SMS, or store-connected automation with eligible ActiveCampaign SMS and sales tools. Neither account name guarantees the required channel, workflow action or CRM entitlement.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox title="Conditional fits" picks={[
          { label: 'Commerce journeys', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Consider when shopping behavior drives email; confirm SMS rollout access and identity requirements.' },
          { label: 'Sales handoff', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Consider when store activity must inform a sales owner; verify SMS, CRM and automation entitlements.' },
          { label: 'Before sending', tool: 'Operator', reason: 'Own permission, inventory truth, purchase exits and reconciliation across every sender.' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Drip</th><th>ActiveCampaign</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Core fit</td><td>Commerce email and behavior-led journeys</td><td>Store journeys connected to sales follow-up where purchased</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS</td><td>Current rollout; confirm account activation and supported audience</td><td>Eligible plan plus SMS add-on and registration</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Identity</td><td>Current SMS page requires an email with a phone number</td><td>Verify Shopify contact matching and channel status mapping</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Wholesale handoff</td><td>Define any external sales-system boundary</td><td>Confirm CRM add-on and exact deal actions</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Send safety</td><td>Test current order, permission and stock checks</td><td>Test current order, permission, deal and stock checks</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Verify channel access before choosing a workflow</h2>
        <p>Drip is no longer accurately described as having no SMS. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/sms">current SMS page</a> describes staged access for existing US paid customers with Support All People enabled, and requires an email alongside each phone number. Confirm approval, supported recipients and sender activation in your account. The same page describes one-way toll-free sending and excludes keywords, short codes and two-way conversations at launch. Do not design a reply-managed drop queue around that scope.</p>
        <p>ActiveCampaign documents SMS as an add-on for eligible plans in its <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/23392070583068-Purchase-the-SMS-add-on">SMS purchasing guide</a>. Confirm country support, sender registration, credits and the exact workflow action before promising a launch date. A subscription to email does not establish SMS eligibility.</p>
        <p>ActiveCampaign is a conditional fit for wholesale approval and rep follow-up when the purchased CRM tools support the required job. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/14251311251356-Overview-of-ActiveCampaign-plans">plan overview</a> separates enhanced CRM add-ons; the <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/218251828-Automation-actions-explained">action reference</a> identifies plan-dependent actions. Verify deal creation, assignment, branching and task handling individually. Drip can fit commerce operators who prefer to keep these sales responsibilities in another system.</p>
        <h2>Shopify truth, identity and permission</h2>
        <p>Use Shopify as the authority for store orders, variants and sellable inventory; use the subscription system for current subscriber status and the CRM for current deal ownership. A marketing segment is a cached decision input, not a stock reservation. ActiveCampaign documents its store connection in the <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/115000202150-Connect-your-Shopify-store-to-ActiveCampaign">Shopify integration guide</a>. Inspect the actual synchronized fields and events in either installed connection before building a purchase or browse branch.</p>
        <p>Map store ID, Shopify customer ID, order ID, variant ID, email and normalized phone to a stable identity. Test guest checkout, changed email, shared phone and merged profiles. Drip's email-required SMS identity means a phone-only waitlist needs an explicit resolution path rather than a fabricated email. Quarantine ambiguous matches instead of sending to both records.</p>
        <p>Maintain email and promotional SMS permissions separately, including purpose, capture source, timestamp and disclosure version. Importing a customer or an accepts-marketing field is not proof of permission for every purpose. Keep suppression history through imports and merges; an old opt-in replay must not overwrite a later opt-out. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent records</a> and set location-aware quiet hours and sender controls for the actual program. Route customer replies only to a supported, staffed destination.</p>
        <h2>Purchase exits and current deal checks</h2>
        <p>Before each queued send, resolve the matching order and journey purpose again. A completed order for the promoted item should exit that item's cart or launch reminder; an unrelated purchase need not end every education journey. A refund or cancellation must not automatically restart promotion without a new eligibility decision. Check subscription status and suppression at send time, including after quiet-hour delays.</p>
        <p>For ActiveCampaign wholesale follow-up, check the current deal ID, status, assigned owner and approval state, rather than any historical won-deal tag. A new order may complete the buying task while a different deal remains open. Record which system cancels the rep task and which cancels marketing reminders. For Drip plus a separate CRM, assign this same reconciliation boundary explicitly; do not assume a webhook stops already queued sends.</p>
        <h2>Proposed merchant tests: launch timing and scarce stock</h2>
        <p>The named scenarios below retain distinct planning ideas from this comparison. They are proposed tests, not verified merchant results or vendor capabilities.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Arena Energy: pair a flavor launch with an athlete collaboration, restricting timing texts to an eligible gym-bag interest segment. Keep the shaker offer in email and test that the text path cannot accidentally inherit that incentive.</li>
          <li>Loom &amp; Frame: disclose edition size and test a sell-through reminder to repeat collectors. Read current available stock before releasing the queue; compare against a withheld eligible collector group.</li>
          <li>Driftwood Surf Co: align a summer boardshort calendar with swell-forecast education. Compare a permissioned restock waitlist with a broader eligible audience without treating forecast interest as SMS permission.</li>
          <li>Noir Collective: schedule a streetwear drop with VIP access before general access. Test whether a restrained calendar preserves incremental contribution and whether a postponed message misses the access window.</li>
          <li>SoleDrop ATX: preserve waitlist priority by size variant. Verify that sold-out sizes cannot trigger general-release reminders, and that a duplicate event cannot issue another early-access link.</li>
          <li>Sauce Lab: expose truthful batch availability before a scarcity reminder. Cancel it if inventory reconciliation invalidates the claim; compare launch-only messaging with a justified reminder.</li>
          <li>Ember Ceramics: use email for the maker story and a permissioned access text for the kiln release. Maintain fulfillment priority independently of send order and delivery latency.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Proposed merchant tests: value, purpose and calendar</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Festival Threads Co: tie city-specific tour merchandise to venue dates and recipient-local send windows. Exclude stale city interests and cap the combined calendar rather than each automation separately.</li>
          <li>Vinyl Vault Records: disclose pressing quantity and test early access for waitlisted repeat collectors. Define value using reconciled net purchases; refunded orders must not inflate collector priority.</li>
          <li>Bloom &amp; Wick: plan holiday teasers and shipping deadlines with gifting and self-purchase segments. Test bundle texts only where permission matches, while scent education remains with the email owner.</li>
          <li>Peak Trail Gear: isolate bargain-sensitive clearance audiences from full-price loyalists. Test the incentive against an eligible holdout instead of asserting that a discount improves or destroys margin.</li>
          <li>Ridge Run Coffee: suppress active subscribers from conflicting flash offers. Test a subscription update arriving during a queued promotion and reconcile before release.</li>
          <li>Cipher Audio: pair advance product education with a launch text to eligible engaged browsers. Compare a no-discount offer with a justified alternative using net contribution, not attributed revenue alone.</li>
          <li>Patchwork Denim: route one-of-one listings to style-preference segments. Stop on purchase and verify that the same item cannot remain eligible in a second profile or delayed branch.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Replay, reconciliation and ownership</h2>
        <p>Test duplicate and out-of-order order events, a delayed opt-out, a missing inventory update and a connection outage. Deduplicate by store, event and journey identity; retain event time as well as receipt time. Replays may repair state but must not resend an expired launch or restore withdrawn permission. Compare source orders and subscription changes against workflow entries, exits and pending sends; investigate discrepancies before resuming.</p>
        <p>Assign an operator to pause the sender, a data owner to resolve unmatched identities and a sales owner to reconcile deals. Keep an exception log for unresolved permissions and stale stock. A calendar owner should resolve collisions across Shopify notifications, email, SMS and rep outreach. If another provider such as <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> owns a specific email job, document that boundary and avoid duplicating the email journeys already in Drip or ActiveCampaign.</p>
        <h2>Cost and decision criteria</h2>
        <p>Request current quotes for the actual eligible audience, SMS segments and carrier charges, plus CRM add-ons, migration and reconciliation effort. Model the peak launch workload and support coverage. See <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>; no fixed price or universal cheaper platform is established here.</p>
        <p>Choose a test window around the buying cycle, sufficient eligible traffic and returns maturity. Randomize comparable eligible customers into treatment and holdout before sending. Calculate net sales after discounts and refunds, then subtract product, fulfillment, payment, message and allocated operating costs once. Compare contribution per eligible customer between groups, deducting shared cohort costs once at cohort level. Report opt-outs, unresolved identities, duplicate sends and inventory failures alongside the financial result.</p>
        <p>Prefer Drip when commerce journeys and its confirmed channel scope meet the team's needs. Prefer ActiveCampaign when verified sales handoffs and purchased workflow capabilities justify the additional administration. Keep either decision conditional on permission integrity, current-state exits and a named operator who can stop the queue.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs ActiveCampaign FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
